Subject: [PATCH] webgl: clamp the WebGL2 uniform and varying limits too

Patch 0026 serves the claimed device's GL limits as min(claim, driver) but
lists only the WebGL1 set plus a handful of WebGL2 ones. With that list served
and the shader precision block corrected by 0029, the reference Windows machine
still disagreed on twelve further parameters, and this adds eleven of them.

They are added from measurement rather than from reading the spec: these are
exactly the ones the V3 diff still showed, and every one reports a number
SwiftShader sets higher than the real Intel GPU does — 60 uniform blocks against
24, 245760 combined components against 212992, 128 varying components against
120. The clamp needs no special casing for that; taking the minimum was already
the rule and these simply were not on the list it consulted.

Three of the sixteen are deliberately left out because min() cannot help and
claiming them would be an over-claim a page can falsify:

  MAX_SAMPLES        reference 16, SwiftShader 4. Allocating a 16x multisample
                     renderbuffer is one call and it would fail.
  MAX_ELEMENT_INDEX  reference 4294967294, ours 1073741823. Read through
                     glGetInteger64v, a different entry point from this hook.
  MAX_TEXTURE_LOD_BIAS  reference 2, ours 15. A GLfloat, read through
                     glGetFloatv, so this integer hook never sees it — and here
                     the reference is *lower*, so it is a clamp we could make
                     rather than a raise we could not.

Those stay red and are honestly red. Raising a limit the rasteriser cannot meet
trades a mismatched number for a failing draw call, which is the worse of the
two.

Ledger: webgl.limits (extends).
diff --git a/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc b/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc
index 136e3ea..9b07c93 100644
--- a/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc
+++ b/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc
@@ -267,6 +267,26 @@ constexpr ApostateGlLimit kApostateGlLimits[] = {
     {GL_MAX_SAMPLES, "MAX_SAMPLES"},
     {GL_MAX_ELEMENTS_INDICES, "MAX_ELEMENTS_INDICES"},
     {GL_MAX_ELEMENTS_VERTICES, "MAX_ELEMENTS_VERTICES"},
+    // WebGL2's uniform-block, varying and shader-interface limits. Added after
+    // measurement rather than by reading the spec: with the list above served
+    // and the shader precision block corrected, these were what the reference
+    // Windows machine still disagreed with, and every one of them reports a
+    // number SwiftShader happens to set higher than the real GPU does. The
+    // clamp handles that on its own — nothing here can over-claim.
+    {GL_MAX_COMBINED_UNIFORM_BLOCKS, "MAX_COMBINED_UNIFORM_BLOCKS"},
+    {GL_MAX_COMBINED_VERTEX_UNIFORM_COMPONENTS,
+     "MAX_COMBINED_VERTEX_UNIFORM_COMPONENTS"},
+    {GL_MAX_COMBINED_FRAGMENT_UNIFORM_COMPONENTS,
+     "MAX_COMBINED_FRAGMENT_UNIFORM_COMPONENTS"},
+    {GL_MAX_UNIFORM_BUFFER_BINDINGS, "MAX_UNIFORM_BUFFER_BINDINGS"},
+    {GL_MAX_VERTEX_UNIFORM_BLOCKS, "MAX_VERTEX_UNIFORM_BLOCKS"},
+    {GL_MAX_FRAGMENT_UNIFORM_BLOCKS, "MAX_FRAGMENT_UNIFORM_BLOCKS"},
+    {GL_MAX_FRAGMENT_UNIFORM_COMPONENTS, "MAX_FRAGMENT_UNIFORM_COMPONENTS"},
+    {GL_MAX_VERTEX_OUTPUT_COMPONENTS, "MAX_VERTEX_OUTPUT_COMPONENTS"},
+    {GL_MAX_FRAGMENT_INPUT_COMPONENTS, "MAX_FRAGMENT_INPUT_COMPONENTS"},
+    {GL_MAX_VARYING_COMPONENTS, "MAX_VARYING_COMPONENTS"},
+    {GL_MAX_TRANSFORM_FEEDBACK_INTERLEAVED_COMPONENTS,
+     "MAX_TRANSFORM_FEEDBACK_INTERLEAVED_COMPONENTS"},
 };
 
 }  // namespace
